[require fileutil::decode [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]
[para]

This package provides low-level commands which help writing decoders
for binary file formats. The package [package zipfile::decode] is built
on it.

[para]

A decoder opens a file, reads its fields one by one, optionally
converts them, and collects them in a result buffer. The package keeps
a single state: one open file, the [term {current value}] (the last
field read, or its converted form), one [term mark] (a remembered file
position) and the [term {result buffer}]. Only one file can be decoded
at a time.

[para]

All commands live in the namespace [namespace ::fileutil::decode].
All of them except [cmd open] and [cmd close] are exported, so a
decoder can import them with

[example {namespace import ::fileutil::decode::*}]

and has to call [cmd open] and [cmd close] by their qualified names.

[section {File access}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::open] [arg fname]]

Opens the file [arg fname] for reading in binary mode. All other
commands operate on this file.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::close]]

Closes the file opened by [cmd open].

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::at]]

Returns the current read position in the file, as an offset in bytes
from its start.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::go] [arg offset]]

Moves the read position to [arg offset] bytes from the start of the
file.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::skip] [arg n]]

Moves the read position [arg n] bytes forward without reading.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::mark]]

Remembers the current read position as the mark, replacing a previous
mark.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::rewind]]

Moves the read position back to the mark and forgets the mark. Throws an
error with the error code [const {FILE DECODE NO MARK}] if there is no
mark.

[list_end]

[section {Reading values}]

Each of these commands reads a field at the current read position,
advances the position, and makes the field the current value.

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::byte]]

Reads one byte as a signed 8-bit integer.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::short-le]]

Reads two bytes as a signed 16-bit integer in little-endian byte
order.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::long-le]]

Reads four bytes as a signed 32-bit integer in little-endian byte
order.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::nbytes] [arg n]]

Reads [arg n] bytes as a binary string.

[list_end]

[section {Working with the current value}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::getval]]

Returns the current value.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::unsigned]]

Converts the current value, as read by [cmd byte], [cmd short-le] or
[cmd long-le], to an unsigned integer of the same width. For example,
the byte [const 0xff] is read by [cmd byte] as [const -1] and becomes
[const 255].

[para]

It is an error, with the error code
[const {FILE DECODE ILLEGAL UNSIGNED}], to use this command after
[cmd nbytes].

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::match] [arg expected]]

Compares the current value with [arg expected]. If they are equal, the
result is [const 1]. Otherwise the read position is moved back to the
mark as by [cmd rewind] and the result is [const 0]. This allows
testing for a signature: set a [cmd mark], read the field, and
[cmd match] it; a mismatch leaves the file where it was before the
field.

[para]

Integers are compared numerically, so [arg expected] can be given in
any integer notation, e.g. hexadecimal. Throws the error of
[cmd rewind] if the values differ and there is no mark.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::recode] [arg cmdprefix]]

Calls the command prefix [arg cmdprefix] with the current value as an
additional argument, in the context of the caller, and makes its
result the new current value.

[list_end]

[section {Result buffer}]

The result buffer is a list of alternating names and values, suitable
for [cmd {array set}] or as a dictionary.

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::clear]]

Empties the result buffer.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::put] [arg name]]

Appends [arg name] and the current value to the result buffer.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::putloc] [arg name]]

Appends [arg name] and the current read position to the result buffer.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::get]]

Returns the contents of the result buffer.

[call [cmd ::fileutil::decode::setbuf] [arg list]]

Replaces the contents of the result buffer with [arg list].

[list_end]

[section EXAMPLE]

A decoder for the header of a BMP image file. It returns the empty
list if the file does not start with the signature [const BM].

[example {
package require fileutil::decode
namespace import ::fileutil::decode::*

proc bmpheader {fname} {
    fileutil::decode::open $fname
    clear
    mark
    nbytes 2
    if {![match BM]} {
        fileutil::decode::close
        return {}
    }
    long-le  ; unsigned ; put filesize
    skip 4                                ; # reserved
    long-le  ; unsigned ; put dataoffset
    long-le  ; unsigned ; put headersize
    long-le  ;            put width
    long-le  ;            put height      ; # negative: top-down
    short-le ; unsigned ; put planes
    short-le ; unsigned ; put bitcount
    fileutil::decode::close
    return [get]
}

puts [bmpheader image.bmp]
}]

For a 2x2 image with 24 bits per pixel this prints

[example {
filesize 70 dataoffset 54 headersize 40 width 2 height 2 planes 1 bitcount 24
}]

